    a text called 'the reap line' is "a number called {'the reap name'} is reap child {'the child name'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the child line', 'the exec branch', 'the stale flag init', 'the stale reap line', 'the stale handler', 'the stale assert', 'the signal flag init', 'the signal line', 'the signal handler', 'the signal assert', 'the reap line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(PRC-79, PRC-86: `Reboot.` lowers to reboot(2), requiring CAP_SYS_BOOT
 (LANGUAGE.md:4520, 4530-4531); on failure (not root, no CAP_SYS_BOOT)
 the error flag is set instead of crashing, `On error` catches it and
 execution continues (LANGUAGE.md:4533-4537) - the exact safety property
 this surface brief's ruling leans on: the campaign runs unprivileged, so
 the kernel refuses the call with EPERM before doing anything, and
 asserting the refusal is itself a memory-safety-relevant test of the
 syscall codegen and the error-flag path, hand-verified this session
 under both pinned compilers.

 REGISTERED THIS BATCH (process batch B, 2026-08-31), `Reboot` ONLY -
 `Shutdown` stays OUT, permanently until Josj rules otherwise. This leaf
 originally drew between `Shutdown` and `Reboot` (process batch A) and
 was left UNREGISTERED because of a master-verified candidate: `Shutdown`
 intermittently BLOCKS INDEFINITELY instead of setting the error flag,
 in an interactive-session context that looks like an authorization
 round-trip (polkit/logind-style) rather than a plain kernel EPERM -
 `/home/josj/scr/english/vox-notes/VERIFIED-SHUTDOWN-BLOCKS-TTY.md`. That
 candidate does not touch `Reboot`, which "always refuses" in the same
 verification matrix, so this batch's surface brief carries the
 corrected policy forward explicitly: "mount/reboot/shutdown emit-safe
 EXCEPT Shutdown (candidate, do not emit Shutdown; Reboot ok)." Dropping
 the `Shutdown` branch entirely (rather than drawing it and hoping the
 hang does not land in a campaign) is what makes this kind safe to
 register at last. `Poweroff`/`Restart`/`Halt` and their own aliases stay
 out of scope and out of the never-emitted guard's narrowing
 (tests/200_never_emitted.vox), pending a wider ruling.)
